Also for the Poincaré inequality: on the Fourier side, for mean-zero functions, `∑' |c_k|² ≤ (1/(4π²)) ∑' (4π²|k|²)|c_k|²` since `|k|² ≥ 1` for k ≠ 0. In the finite-support setting this is elementary. Fully unconditionally provable for finitely-supported coefficient families, and for ℓ² via tsum. Great: Poincaré becomes `∑ |c k|² ≤ (1/(2π))² * ∑ (4π² |k|²) |c k|²` using `1 ≤ |k|²`. Wait (1/2π)² * 4π²|k|² = |k|² ≥ 1. Yes. So ‖u‖₂ ≤ (1/2π)‖∇u‖₂ where ‖∇u‖² = ∑ 4π²|k|²|c_k|². Good.

For Sobolev embedding: H^s ↪ C⁰ for s > n/2: `‖u‖_∞ ≤ C ‖u‖_{H^s}` with `C = (∑_k (1+|k|²)^{-s})^{1/2}`. Need summability of `(1+|k|²)^{-s/2}`^2 = `(1+|k|²)^{-s}` over ℤⁿ for s > n/2. Prove via dyadic shell bounds or integral comparison. This is the hard analytic part in Lean.

Approach for summability: Bound `(1+|k|²)^{-s} ≤ ...` and compare with an integral. Options:
1. Show `∑_{k ∈ ℤⁿ} (1+|k|²)^{-s} < ∞` using `Summable` of a comparable function and a domination by an integral over ℝⁿ: `(1+|k|²)^{-s} ≤ ∫_{cube around k} (1+|x|²/...)^{-s}`... Standard: on the unit cube centered at k, `|x| ≤ |k| + √n/2`, so `1+|k|² ≤ ...`. Hmm, easier: `(1+|k|²)^{-s} ≤ C ∫_{[k-1/2,k+1/2]^n} (1+|x|²)^{-s} dx` where on the cube `|x| ≥ |k| - √n/2` so `1+|x|² ≥ 1 + (|k|-r)²_+`... Then need `(1+(m-r)²)^{-s} ≥ c (1+m²)^{-s}`, which holds with c depending on r. That gives domination by a constant times the integral, and the integral converges for s > n/2.

But in Lean, integrating over ℝⁿ with a radial-ish function is nontrivial. Instead, **dyadic shell bounds** are more elementary and well-suited to Lean: 
- Inner part: finitely many terms (`|k| ≤ 1`... using `Finset` over `Finset.Icc`).
- Shell `2^j ≤ |k| < 2^{j+1}`: number of lattice points `≤ (2^{j+1}+1)^n ≤ C·2^{jn}`, each term `≤ (1+2^{2j})^{-s} ≤ 2^{-2js}`, product `≤ C 2^{j(n-2s)}`, summable geometrically when n - 2s < 0, i.e., s > n/2.

